# Draft registration for the ADR 0005 proposal. A Decision vertex is created
# only after human acceptance and merge, never while this plan remains draft.
"@context": "urn:bedrock:context/v1"
"@id": "urn:bedrock:vertex/plan-batched-deletion-primitive"
"@type": "urn:bedrock:ontology/Plan"
label: "Plan: propose typed S3 multi-object deletion"
intent: >
  Carry ADR 0005 through adversarial review and human acceptance, then — only
  under a separately authorized kernel-extension batch — deliver an additive
  AtomicKeyspace primitive for 1,000-key S3 DeleteObjects transport with honest
  per-key partial outcomes and unchanged single-key atomicity law.
acceptanceCriteria:
  - "A human accepts and merges ADR 0005; before acceptance, no Decision vertex or accepted-log entry represents it as closed law."
  - "The API admits at most 100,000 unique keys, sends 1,000-key sequential chunks, retains only chunk-local paths, caps each diagnostic at 512 bytes, and shares request-level instances."
  - "The existing loopback bulk branch gains key-vector logging, key-less occurrence arms, configurable entry errors, whole-request cuts, and 405/501 refusal mode."
  - "Unconfirmed stops label only their untouched tail NotAttempted; a mid-call definitive refusal labels its current chunk and untouched tail Unsupported."
  - "Invalid, incomplete, contradictory, or non-bijective responses never default an omitted key to success and never trigger per-key fallback."
  - "The outcome vector preserves classification for bounded policy; remaining only serializes unresolved keys for durable checkpoints."
  - "Deletion writes no tombstone or counter; fence-blind batch delete plus same-incarnation/version-0 recreate can reuse swept chunk paths, the named ADR 0004 residual."
  - "Published delete_many/delete/delete_if_match behavior remains unchanged; no batch is transactional or conditionally guarded."
  - "A36-A45 pass in CI and a real-S3 witness proves checksum-compatible exact response bijection; refutation blocks publication."
  - "The primitive uses caller-supplied keys only; LIST authority, durable job checkpoints, and epoch adoption remain outside it."
consumes:
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/invariant-kernel-extension"
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/invariant-13-state"
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/invariant-08-dependency-boundary"
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/structure-kernel"
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/decision-0001-atomic-keyspace"
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/decision-0002-streams"
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/decision-0004-streaming-value-io-v2"
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/decision-0005-batched-deletion"
  - "urn:bedrock:path/crates/yeetz-s3-kernel"
  - "urn:bedrock:path/crates/yeetz-sdk-s3"
tasks:
  - "Keep ADR 0005 explicitly proposed and register it only as this draft plan."
  - "Run the independent adversarial review and amend the proposal forward where required."
  - "Obtain human acceptance and merge; only then register ADR 0005 as a Decision and add it to the accepted structure log."
  - "Obtain separate human authorization for one firewalled kernel implementation batch."
  - "Implement the bounded direct-SDK seam and additive AtomicKeyspace API without migrating or strengthening existing methods."
  - "Extend the existing loopback bulk branch with key-vector logging, key-less occurrence arms, configurable entry errors, request cuts, and refusal mode; prove A36-A45 and real-S3 qualification."
  - "Publish the next free 0.4.x closure release; orchestrate every downstream adoption separately."
witnesses:
  - "https://github.com/cleverunicornz/yeetz-s3-kernel/actions/runs/32665465723"
  - "https://github.com/cleverunicornz/yeetz-s3-kernel/actions/runs/32736208926"
reflectDepth: 2
oracle: "urn:bedrock:vertex/decision-0005-batched-deletion"
disposition:
  state: done
  residual: >
    The promise closed end to end: ADR 0005 accepted and registered
    (PRs #33/#34), implemented and proven (PR #35; nitpick #36), and
    published as 0.4.2 — the next free 0.4.x, 0.4.1 having been
    consumed pre-implementation — release PR #37 (94c7b15, gates run
    32736208926), all four crates verified on index.crates.io
    2026-08-24T14:03Z. Downstream adoption (yeetz sealed-epoch
    deletion, certified trim, chunk GC) stays each consumer's own
    orchestration; the standing kernel residuals live with the ADR
    and its Decision vertex.
body: |
  ## Proposal depth

  The complete semantics, Rust API sketches, alternatives, release plan, and
  A36-A45 oracle are carried verbatim in the body of Decision vertex
  decision-0005-batched-deletion. This vertex
  routes that proposal through the execution graph; it does not close the fork
  and does not authorize implementation.

  ## Authorization gates

  Human acceptance and merge come first. The acceptance-time change writes the
  Decision vertex and extends the accepted ADR structure log. A second explicit
  human authorization is then required before the kernel implementation batch.
  Downstream yeetz epoch adoption remains a separate orchestration after the
  assured crate release.

  ## Reflection depth

  Depth 2 means reflection reads both this plan/ADR and the independent review,
  then checks the implementation witnesses and declared residuals rather than
  treating a green design-branch gate as proof that the primitive exists.

  ## Closure record

  The full publication facts, kept out of the compiled face: ADR 0005
  was accepted by human merge of PR #33 (60cb5c35) after the two-round
  adversarial review, registered as a Decision vertex by PR #34
  (1ffe12d), implemented with the A36-A45 contract suite and the
  real-S3 DeleteObjects leg by PR #35 (8d7f3e8), with the seam-totality
  nitpick closed by PR #36 (51294a1). The closure shipped as **0.4.2**
  — not 0.4.1, which had been consumed at 2026-08-23T08:49Z
  pre-implementation, discovered via the sparse index during the
  publish — as release PR #37 (merge 94c7b15), gates run
  32736208926 green, all four crates published bottom-up
  (yeetz-sdk-core, yeetz-sdk-s3, yeetz-s3-kernel requiring
  yeetz-sdk-s3 ^0.4.2, yeetz-s3-streams) and verified on
  index.crates.io at 2026-08-24T14:03Z. Task 7's "next free 0.4.x" is
  therefore satisfied exactly. What remains open is downstream
  adoption — the yeetz forge's sealed-epoch delete worker, certified
  trim sweeps, and chunk GC migrating onto `delete_objects` — each
  under its own contracts and authorization, never this plan's to
  assure. The standing kernel residuals (caller-enforced ADR 0004
  quiescence with same-era chunk-path reuse, the weaker legacy
  response-trust path, caller-owned retry classification) live with
  the ADR and its Decision vertex, not with this plan.
